  "text": "Here's a URL shortener that works: a load balancer, two stateless app servers, a cache and a key-value store, sized for about thirty-five thousand redirects a second at peak. Now give it ten times the traffic, three hundred and fifty thousand a second, and every box has to answer for it. Twelve questions: nine worked through on this diagram, two quick ones, and a news feed to close.\n\nQuestion one. Horizontal versus vertical scaling, which do you reach for? Vertical is a bigger machine: simple, but it has a ceiling, and it's still one machine that can fail. Horizontal is more machines behind the load balancer: nearly unlimited, and redundant, but only if any server can handle any request. So keep the app tier stateless, with session state in a shared cache or database. These redirect servers already are, so ten times the traffic means more of them. How many more? Enough that none runs near full. In the simplest queue model, a request waits one service time on average at fifty percent load, nine at ninety percent, and ninety-nine at ninety-nine. Headroom is what the extra servers buy.",
